WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.294 --> 00:00:04.801
Holmes wanted to walk on the moor before it got dark, and I agreed to accompany him.

2
00:00:04.802 --> 00:00:08.547
"I think I'll just put this horseshoe in my pocket for luck," he said.

3
00:00:08.548 --> 00:00:14.632
Colonel Ross, who'd shown signs of impatience at Holmes' quiet, systematic methods, gave an irritated sigh.

4
00:00:14.633 --> 00:00:20.133
"Please come back to Straker's house with me, Inspector Gregory. There are several points on which I'd like your advice."

5
00:00:20.134 --> 00:00:23.963
The colonel bowed to us, and then he and Gregory turned back toward the house.

6
00:00:23.963 --> 00:00:28.528
The sun was beginning to sink and the sloping plain in front of us was bathed in gold.

7
00:00:28.529 --> 00:00:32.711
But the glories of the landscape were wasted on Holmes, who was deep in thought.

8
00:00:32.712 --> 00:00:38.458
At last he broke his silence and suggested that we focus on Silver Blaze instead of the murder.

9
00:00:38.459 --> 00:00:45.672
"A horse is a very sociable animal, Watson," said Holmes. "Assuming he broke away during the murder, where would he go?

10
00:00:45.673 --> 00:00:51.505
He'd either return to his own stable or go over to Mapleton. Why should he run wild upon the moor?"

11
00:00:51.506 --> 00:00:54.513
"Maybe the Gypsies kidnapped him," I suggested.

12
00:00:54.514 --> 00:01:00.418
"No, they always clear out when they hear of trouble because they don't want to be pestered by the police," explained Holmes.

13
00:01:00.419 --> 00:01:06.664
"So it would be too risky for them to try to sell such a horse. The Gypsies would have gained nothing by taking him."

14
00:01:06.665 --> 00:01:08.751
"Then where is Silver Blaze?"

15
00:01:08.752 --> 00:01:18.334
"He's not at Colonel Ross' stable, so he must be at Mapleton," said Holmes. "Let's assume that for now. You can see that the moor dips toward Mapleton.

16
00:01:18.335 --> 00:01:25.239
That long hollow over there must have been very wet on Monday night. I'm willing to bet that we'll find some tracks over there."

17
00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:29.986
We had been walking briskly during our conversation, and in a few minutes we reached the hollow.

18
00:01:29.987 --> 00:01:34.399
At Holmes' request I walked down the bank to the right while he went to the left.

19
00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:38.639
I had not walked more than fifty paces before he gave a shout and waved to me.

20
00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:43.060
When I reached him, I plainly saw the tracks of a horse in the mud in front of him.

21
00:01:43.061 --> 00:01:47.125
The horseshoe that he removed from his pocket matched the impression on the ground.

22
00:01:47.126 --> 00:01:56.469
"See the value of imagination?" said Holmes. "This is the one quality that Gregory lacks. We imagined where the horse might go, and we were right."

23
00:01:56.470 --> 00:02:04.558
We continued to walk toward Mapleton, sometimes losing the horse's tracks in the hard, dry dirt, but picking them up again where it was wet.

24
00:02:04.559 --> 00:02:12.121
When we were quite close to the stable, we found the tracks once again. Only this time a man's tracks were visible next to the horse's.

25
00:02:12.122 --> 00:02:14.943
"The horse was alone before!" I cried.

26
00:02:14.944 --> 00:02:19.348
"Quite so. But what is this?" Holmes pointed at the ground.

27
00:02:19.349 --> 00:02:24.102
The tracks of man and horse turned sharply and headed back toward Colonel Ross' stable.

28
00:02:24.103 --> 00:02:30.123
As we followed the tracks, Holmes kept his eyes on the trail. I happened to look a little to one side.

29
00:02:30.124 --> 00:02:35.091
"Look, Holmes!" I cried. "They turned around again and headed toward Mapleton."

30
00:02:35.092 --> 00:02:38.759
"Very good, Watson! Let's follow those tracks."

31
00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:42.680
We did not have far to go because the tracks soon led us to Mapleton stable.

32
00:02:42.681 --> 00:02:48.500
As we approached, a fierce-looking elderly man strode through the gate with a riding crop in his hand.

33
00:02:48.501 --> 00:02:54.000
"We don't want any loiterers around here." The old man swung the riding crop menacingly.

34
00:02:54.001 --> 00:03:00.572
"Would you be Silas Brown, the manager?" asked Holmes in his sweetest voice. "I only need ten minutes of your time."

35
00:03:00.573 --> 00:03:04.913
"I've no time for strangers. Be off or I'll set the dog on you!"

36
00:03:04.914 --> 00:03:11.158
Holmes leaned forward and whispered something in the trainer's ear. Brown started violently and his face flushed.

37
00:03:11.159 --> 00:03:15.404
"It's a lie!" shouted the trainer. "A complete lie!"

38
00:03:15.405 --> 00:03:20.494
"Shall we argue about it here in public or talk it over in your parlor?" asked Holmes.

39
00:03:20.495 --> 00:03:22.391
"Oh, come in if you must."

40
00:03:22.392 --> 00:03:26.058
"I'm sure you don't mind waiting out here for me, Watson," said Holmes.

41
00:03:26.059 --> 00:03:30.051
It was twenty minutes before Holmes and the trainer reappeared.

42
00:03:30.052 --> 00:03:38.798
Such a change had come over Silas Brown! His face was pale, beads of perspiration shone on his brow, and his hands shook with nervousness.

43
00:03:38.799 --> 00:03:42.457
"Your instructions will be carried out," said Brown to the detective.

44
00:03:42.458 --> 00:03:47.536
"Make sure you carry them out exactly," said Holmes with a menacing tone in his voice.

45
00:03:47.537 --> 00:03:51.036
Holmes and I began to trudge back toward Colonel Ross' stable.

46
00:03:51.037 --> 00:03:57.533
"I've never met a more perfect combination of bully, coward, and sneak than Silas Brown," said Holmes.

47
00:03:57.534 --> 00:03:59.367
"So he has the horse?"

48
00:03:59.368 --> 00:04:08.355
"Oh yes. He tried to deny it at first, but there was no denying that his square-toed boots perfectly matched the impressions in the mud," said Holmes.

49
00:04:08.356 --> 00:04:16.767
"I described to him how he must have seen the horse on the moor early Tuesday morning. His first instinct was to return Silver Blaze to Colonel Ross.

50
00:04:16.768 --> 00:04:22.680
But then he realized that if he hid the horse until the Wessex Cup race was over, his own horse might win."

51
00:04:22.681 --> 00:04:26.446
"Didn't Gregory say that the police searched Brown's stable?" I asked.

52
00:04:26.446 --> 00:04:30.168
"Yes, but a crafty old schemer like Brown has lots of tricks."

53
00:04:30.169 --> 00:04:32.922
"Aren't you afraid that Brown will hurt Silver Blaze?"

54
00:04:32.923 --> 00:04:36.417
"He knows that his only chance of mercy is to keep the horse safe."

55
00:04:36.418 --> 00:04:42.075
I shook my head. "Colonel Ross doesn't seem like the kind of person who would show mercy in a case like this."

56
00:04:42.076 --> 00:04:48.229
"It's not Colonel Ross that Brown has to worry about," said Holmes. "I'm the person who will determine his fate.

57
00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:56.629
That is the advantage of not being an official police detective. I don't know if you've noticed, Watson, but the colonel has been just a bit disrespectful toward me.

58
00:04:56.630 --> 00:04:59.391
I'm going to have a little fun at his expense."

59
00:04:59.392 --> 00:05:03.891
Now that we'd found Silver Blaze, I was sure we would concentrate on finding the murderer.

60
00:05:03.892 --> 00:05:09.135
But when we arrived back at Straker's house, Holmes announced that we would return to London that night.

61
00:05:09.136 --> 00:05:14.048
"So you've given up on arresting the murderer of poor Straker?" said Colonel Ross with a sneer.

62
00:05:14.049 --> 00:05:22.305
Holmes shrugged his shoulders. "It's a difficult case, but I have every hope that your horse will run the race. Do you have a photograph of Straker?"

63
00:05:22.306 --> 00:05:25.138
Gregory handed him a photograph from an envelope.

64
00:05:25.139 --> 00:05:29.948
"I want to ask the maid another question before we leave for the train station," said Holmes.

65
00:05:29.949 --> 00:05:36.282
"I must say that I'm disappointed in our London detective," said Colonel Ross when Holmes was out of earshot.

66
00:05:36.283 --> 00:05:39.778
"We're not any further along on this case than before he came."

67
00:05:39.779 --> 00:05:47.198
I was about to say something in Holmes' defense when he reappeared. As we stepped into the carriage, a groom held the door open for us.

68
00:05:47.199 --> 00:05:52.780
"I see you keep sheep," Holmes said to the boy. "Have you noticed anything wrong with them lately?"

69
00:05:52.781 --> 00:05:55.264
"Only that three of them have gone lame, sir."

70
00:05:55.265 --> 00:06:00.273
"Gregory, I suggest you pay attention to that," said Holmes as the boy closed the coach door.

71
00:06:00.274 --> 00:06:04.249
Gregory looked puzzled. "Is there anything else I should notice?"

72
00:06:04.250 --> 00:06:08.687
"The curious incident of the dog in the nighttime," said Holmes.

73
00:06:08.688 --> 00:06:11.004
"The dog did nothing in the nighttime."

74
00:06:11.005 --> 00:06:16.500
"That was the curious incident," remarked the great detective.

